==English==

===Etymology===
Introduced by {{w|H. W. Fowler}} and {{w|Francis George Fowler|F. G. Fowler}} in ''{{w|The King's English}}'' (1906).

===Noun===
{{examples|'''Tony Blair''' visited the town today. '''The Prime Minister''' saw the new theatre. '''The British leader''' left in the afternoon.}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|journalism|literature}} The use of [[synonym]]s to avoid repetition (regarded as a stylistic fault when done excessively).
#: {{syn|en|inelegant variation}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|author=Peter M. Tiersma|title=Legal Language|publisher=University of Chicago Press|isbn=9780226803029|page=131|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Sq8XXTo3A48C&pg=PA131&dq=%22elegant+variation%22&hl=&cd=14&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22elegant%20variation%22&f=false|text=A closely related rule for encoding legal texts is the ''same meaning, same form'' principle. We saw previously that this rule requires lawyers to avoid '''elegant variation'''. Instead, they should consistently use one word if they mean the same thing.}}

